from folks like yourself.

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:27.646
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:27.646 --> 00:00:30.326
please go to khanacademy.org/donate

00:00:30.326 --> 00:00:34.216
and donations of all levels
make a huge, huge difference.

00:00:34.216 --> 00:00:38.066
Every dollar donated to Khan
Academy creates many hundreds

00:00:38.066 --> 00:00:40.276
of dollars of social impact.

00:00:40.276 --> 00:00:41.952
One of the most scalable
ways that you can create

00:00:41.952 --> 00:00:44.216
social impact in the world.

00:00:44.216 --> 00:00:47.736
Also I wanna give a special
shout out to corporations

00:00:47.736 --> 00:00:50.276
that have stepped up, especially
during the COVID crisis.

00:00:50.276 --> 00:00:53.676
Realizing that as the load
on Khan Academy has increased

00:00:53.676 --> 00:00:56.456
by a factor of two or three,
our costs have as well.

00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:58.706
We wanted to accelerate a
bunch of programs to support

00:00:58.706 --> 00:01:01.226
parents, teachers, and
students, so special thanks

00:01:01.226 --> 00:01:04.196
to Bank of America,
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Fastly

00:01:04.196 --> 00:01:06.806
and Novartis for stepping up
and helping us fill this gap.

00:01:06.806 --> 00:01:09.176
But once again, we still need help,

00:01:09.176 --> 00:01:13.896
especially as we go into
2021 to fill that gap.

00:01:13.896 --> 00:01:16.516
Last but not least remind
everyone that there's a version

00:01:16.516 --> 00:01:20.156
of this live stream that
you can get in podcast form.

00:01:20.156 --> 00:01:23.366
Homeroom with Sal, The
Podcast, so check that out

00:01:23.366 --> 00:01:26.086
wherever you get your podcasts.

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:29.826
With that said, I'm excited
to introduce Brooke Mabry,

00:01:29.826 --> 00:01:32.333
am I pronouncing Mabry as Mabry, or Mabry?

00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:34.743
- No you got it right Sal, Mabry.

00:01:34.743 --> 00:01:38.099
- Mabry, all right, well,
Brooke thanks for joining us.

00:01:40.347 --> 00:01:43.276
You are a Strategic Content
Design Coordinator at NWEA,

00:01:44.666 --> 00:01:47.036
and NWEA is one of our closest partners

00:01:47.036 --> 00:01:47.916
here at Khan Academy.

00:01:47.916 --> 00:01:51.306
But why don't we, for those
folks who don't know what NWEA

00:01:51.306 --> 00:01:53.536
is or what a strategic
design coordinator is,

00:01:53.536 --> 00:01:55.546
maybe that's a good place to start.

00:01:55.546 --> 00:01:58.506
- Absolutely, so NWEA is a nonprofit,

00:01:58.506 --> 00:02:00.956
educational organization
that's really grounded

00:02:00.956 --> 00:02:04.336
in our mission of partnering
to help all kids learn.

00:02:04.336 --> 00:02:08.016
And so we work in all 50
States and 140 plus countries

00:02:08.016 --> 00:02:10.976
around the world, providing
assessment solutions

00:02:10.976 --> 00:02:13.036
and educational solutions, to really think

00:02:13.036 --> 00:02:15.696
about how we can harness student learning

00:02:15.696 --> 00:02:18.936
and grow those students as
far forward as possible.

00:02:18.936 --> 00:02:22.596
My role in the organization is
on the professional learning

00:02:22.596 --> 00:02:26.366
design team, and so my
work is really around

00:02:26.366 --> 00:02:30.336
how do we create content
that supports teachers,

00:02:30.336 --> 00:02:32.226
helping their students to learn?

00:02:32.226 --> 00:02:34.966
How do we help them
use the assessment data

00:02:34.966 --> 00:02:37.946
that they get from our
flagship product, map growth,

00:02:37.946 --> 00:02:40.926
but also how do we deepen
their formative assessment

00:02:40.926 --> 00:02:43.416
practices and their assessment literacy.

00:02:43.416 --> 00:02:46.586
So, specifically around strategic content,

00:02:46.586 --> 00:02:48.809
that's really our largest partnerships.

00:02:50.377 --> 00:02:52.736
We're both involved
together with Clark County

00:02:52.736 --> 00:02:55.216
school district and Las Vegas.

00:02:55.216 --> 00:02:58.626
And we also work with New
York city public schools

00:02:58.626 --> 00:02:59.856
and Chicago public schools.

00:02:59.856 --> 00:03:03.566
So, I'm really in there to
help support our consultants

00:03:03.566 --> 00:03:06.026
and our teams who partner with
some of our largest districts

00:03:06.026 --> 00:03:07.173
across the nation.

00:03:08.181 --> 00:03:11.566
- And just to remind, a lot of folks NWEA,

00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:13.556
they may or may not know
that, but the administrators

00:03:13.556 --> 00:03:15.526
as you just said of the
map growth assessment,

00:03:15.526 --> 00:03:18.066
which 20% of all kids in America take.

00:03:18.066 --> 00:03:20.966
And if you look, there's
been over 50 efficacy studies

00:03:20.966 --> 00:03:22.296
on Khan Academy and most of them

00:03:22.296 --> 00:03:25.196
have been using your assessment,
the map growth assessment.

00:03:25.196 --> 00:03:27.546
It's a real differentiator,
it's really the first

00:03:27.546 --> 00:03:30.206
and still the major assessment
that can measure growth,

00:03:30.206 --> 00:03:32.706
so a lot of assessments
just measure where you are,

00:03:32.706 --> 00:03:34.096
or are you at grade level,

00:03:34.096 --> 00:03:37.746
but this measures where you
are and how much you grew

00:03:37.746 --> 00:03:39.446
from one point to another point.

00:03:39.446 --> 00:03:41.896
And how does that compare to
how much other people grow?

00:03:41.896 --> 00:03:43.796
Which is really valuable because we know

00:03:43.796 --> 00:03:46.326
there might be a sixth
grader who started the year

00:03:46.326 --> 00:03:50.086
at a fourth grade level and
got to a fifth grade level

00:03:50.086 --> 00:03:51.086
and they had a year of growth,

00:03:51.086 --> 00:03:53.576
but on a sixth grade assessment,
they might not see a lot.

00:03:53.576 --> 00:03:55.696
Likewise, you might have a
sixth grader who starts the year

00:03:55.696 --> 00:03:58.856
at seventh grade level and
stays at a seventh grade level.

00:03:58.856 --> 00:04:00.716
They'll do great on the
grade level assessment,

00:04:00.716 --> 00:04:02.786
but they saw no growth,
which would be a problem.

00:04:02.786 --> 00:04:05.326
So that's what's really cool
about the map growth assessment

00:04:05.326 --> 00:04:09.326
and why it's so powerful to
use it to measure efficacy

00:04:09.326 --> 00:04:10.606
on things like Khan Academy.

00:04:10.606 --> 00:04:12.506
And as we just saw in the screenshot,

00:04:12.506 --> 00:04:15.716
we have this partnership where
now the map growth assessment

00:04:15.716 --> 00:04:17.926
acts as a placement and a diagnostic

00:04:17.926 --> 00:04:20.946
for personalized practice on Khan Academy.

00:04:20.946 --> 00:04:23.836
This Map Accelerator which we're
doing with school districts

00:04:23.836 --> 00:04:27.766
like Clark County, which is
Las Vegas and many others.

00:04:27.766 --> 00:04:30.346
Many hundreds of thousands
of students are now using

00:04:30.346 --> 00:04:32.916
this inside of their classroom,

00:04:32.916 --> 00:04:34.236
in conjunction with their teachers.

00:04:34.236 --> 00:04:35.886
And it's what's connecting the assessment,

00:04:35.886 --> 00:04:38.296
the assessment helps figure
out what you need to work on,

00:04:38.296 --> 00:04:41.146
on Khan Academy and then,
because everything is linked,

00:04:41.146 --> 00:04:43.726
you can figure out how
much that Khan Academy work

00:04:43.726 --> 00:04:46.276
has driven hopefully, and we expect better

00:04:46.276 --> 00:04:47.196
than expected growth.

00:04:47.196 --> 00:04:48.977
So it's a really exciting partnership

00:04:48.977 --> 00:04:50.826
that we're doing together.

00:04:50.826 --> 00:04:54.196
So, you know, we're in very
unusual circumstances right now,

00:04:54.196 --> 00:04:57.276
I think what's is what month,
eight or nine of COVID.

00:04:57.276 --> 00:05:02.276
And I know NWEA has been
studying what the effect of COVID

00:05:02.406 --> 00:05:04.226
has been or what it could be,

00:05:04.226 --> 00:05:06.936
and also how people
have been transitioning

00:05:06.936 --> 00:05:09.446
or not to distance learning.

00:05:09.446 --> 00:05:11.986
What have you been seeing
both on the research side,

00:05:11.986 --> 00:05:14.476
in terms of how it's
affecting students performance

00:05:14.476 --> 00:05:16.899
and other ways that
people are trying to cope?

00:05:18.136 --> 00:05:20.206
- Well, if you've read
our latest research brief,

00:05:20.206 --> 00:05:23.056
we're cautiously optimistic
about what we found.

00:05:23.056 --> 00:05:25.546
We made some projections
back in the spring

00:05:25.546 --> 00:05:27.686
based on our summer learning loss data

00:05:27.686 --> 00:05:30.466
that we have accumulated over the years.

00:05:30.466 --> 00:05:33.136
But when we looked at this
fall data and really compared

00:05:33.136 --> 00:05:35.836
what was happening, we didn't
quite see the same slides

00:05:35.836 --> 00:05:38.736
that we projected and
that's really good news.

00:05:38.736 --> 00:05:42.916
Reading was around typical
of where we typically see

00:05:42.916 --> 00:05:44.386
students coming in the fall,

00:05:44.386 --> 00:05:48.336
we did see five to 10
percentile points of a drop

00:05:48.336 --> 00:05:51.966
in the mass area, but it's
not as deep as we thought

00:05:51.966 --> 00:05:54.136
it would be, so we really
want to give a shout out

00:05:54.136 --> 00:05:56.196
to teachers and say, you really stepped up

00:05:56.196 --> 00:06:00.816
and you really adapted well
and found ways to lean in

00:06:00.816 --> 00:06:04.053
and make sure that that
loss was not as large

00:06:04.053 --> 00:06:05.516
as we thought it might be.

00:06:05.516 --> 00:06:07.136
So, that's the initial results,

00:06:07.136 --> 00:06:10.606
but we do still have some places

00:06:10.606 --> 00:06:13.736
where we don't fully
understand what's happening

00:06:13.736 --> 00:06:15.776
in the data, because there
are missing students.

00:06:15.776 --> 00:06:19.326
We do have about a quarter of students

00:06:19.326 --> 00:06:22.696
that are typically represented
in our data, not there.

00:06:22.696 --> 00:06:26.026
And what we have found
is those are our students

00:06:26.026 --> 00:06:28.446
who come from the most
underserved communities,

00:06:28.446 --> 00:06:31.966
students who tend to be students who live

00:06:31.966 --> 00:06:33.616
in impoverished areas,

00:06:33.616 --> 00:06:36.986
students who tended to
start at a lower percentile

00:06:36.986 --> 00:06:39.306
to begin with and our students of color.

00:06:39.306 --> 00:06:44.306
So, we don't have any real conclusions,

00:06:45.676 --> 00:06:47.776
it's too early right now
to make any conclusions

00:06:47.776 --> 00:06:49.276
in that area, but we do wanna say

00:06:49.276 --> 00:06:52.306
that right now we're seeing some positives

00:06:52.306 --> 00:06:54.936
and some things to hang our hope on,

00:06:54.936 --> 00:06:58.456
but we also know that we
wanted to understand deeper

00:06:58.456 --> 00:07:01.579
what's happening in our
places where students

00:07:01.579 --> 00:07:03.649
have tended to be underserved.

00:07:04.546 --> 00:07:08.006
- So to make sure I'm understanding
this, what we're seeing,

00:07:08.006 --> 00:07:09.876
at least based on the data you're seeing,

00:07:09.876 --> 00:07:13.166
the reading loss is not
as much as expected,

00:07:13.166 --> 00:07:15.256
if anything, it looks like
your data's showing that kids

00:07:15.256 --> 00:07:17.836
are tracking similar to
what they would have done

00:07:17.836 --> 00:07:19.106
in a normal year.

00:07:19.106 --> 00:07:21.966
Math, there is a bit of a regression

00:07:21.966 --> 00:07:25.106
compared to a normal year, and
then there's a big asterisk

00:07:25.106 --> 00:07:28.056
on all of this because
the number of students

00:07:28.056 --> 00:07:30.566
that you can assess,
there's been some percentage

00:07:30.566 --> 00:07:33.736
of the population, whether it's
five, 10% of the population

00:07:33.736 --> 00:07:37.336
that is just not engaging right
now, over distance learning.

00:07:37.336 --> 00:07:40.313
And so you couldn't assess them right now,

00:07:40.313 --> 00:07:42.666
but there's a lot of reason to believe

00:07:42.666 --> 00:07:46.606
that those are the kids that
were already maybe facing

00:07:46.606 --> 00:07:49.016
not as fast growth as we would hope.

00:07:49.016 --> 00:07:51.156
The fact that they haven't
been engaged in their schools

00:07:51.156 --> 00:07:54.831
would argue that there,
they're probably the worst off

00:07:54.831 --> 00:07:56.549
in terms of growth of their learning

00:07:56.549 --> 00:07:58.866
and they're probably atrophying.

00:07:58.866 --> 00:08:01.056
And because that's not in the data,

00:08:01.056 --> 00:08:04.596
it might be making the data
seem a little bit less negative

00:08:04.596 --> 00:08:06.026
than it might be.

00:08:06.026 --> 00:08:09.776
- That is very possible, and
that's absolutely where we're

00:08:09.776 --> 00:08:11.806
standing right now and hoping

00:08:11.806 --> 00:08:15.356
that we'll have a more
complete dataset as we progress

00:08:15.356 --> 00:08:16.206
through the year.

00:08:17.393 --> 00:08:18.576
- Are you seeing that?

00:08:18.576 --> 00:08:20.856
Are you seeing that there
is five or 10% of students

00:08:20.856 --> 00:08:22.146
that have just not been showing up?

00:08:22.146 --> 00:08:24.806
Are districts getting
better at finding them

00:08:24.806 --> 00:08:27.956
when you do your January
map growth administration,

00:08:27.956 --> 00:08:29.856
do you think you're gonna
get more of those kids

00:08:29.856 --> 00:08:30.979
in the assessment?

00:08:32.111 --> 00:08:35.106
- I think it's that, but it's
also, some of our partners

00:08:35.106 --> 00:08:37.006
decided not to test remotely.

00:08:37.006 --> 00:08:40.596
They were busy trying to
up-level their remote instruction

00:08:40.596 --> 00:08:43.386
and just didn't feel that
they were ready to administer

00:08:43.386 --> 00:08:45.346
the test remotely, we had a large turnout

00:08:45.346 --> 00:08:48.506
for remote testing, but we
did have fewer districts

00:08:48.506 --> 00:08:52.676
who made a choice not to
test their whole district,

00:08:52.676 --> 00:08:53.509
but you're right.

00:08:53.509 --> 00:08:55.526
There is a percentage
of students out there

00:08:55.526 --> 00:08:57.676
who have not been engaged in school

00:08:57.676 --> 00:09:00.016
and have not obviously been engaged

00:09:00.016 --> 00:09:01.896
in the assessment portion too.

00:09:01.896 --> 00:09:05.426
And I think anecdotally, we're
hearing from organizations

00:09:05.426 --> 00:09:07.046
that there's a real struggle,

00:09:07.046 --> 00:09:09.466
I think the technological
struggle, if you look

00:09:09.466 --> 00:09:12.736
at how we've defined interrupted learning

00:09:12.736 --> 00:09:16.226
to be about the physical
space, the technological space,

00:09:16.226 --> 00:09:20.506
the social emotional, the cognitive,

00:09:20.506 --> 00:09:25.506
but that technological divide
is real in our country.

00:09:25.706 --> 00:09:28.366
And so I know that, that
is certainly something

00:09:28.366 --> 00:09:31.996
that the district that my son belongs to.

00:09:31.996 --> 00:09:33.336
I know that I have teacher friends,

00:09:33.336 --> 00:09:35.576
who've said that they have difficulty

00:09:35.576 --> 00:09:36.836
getting a hold of students

00:09:36.836 --> 00:09:40.116
and students have difficulty
accessing internet.

00:09:40.116 --> 00:09:42.446
So, I think that there
are a number of things

00:09:42.446 --> 00:09:44.946
that are happening that are
preventing those pieces,

00:09:44.946 --> 00:09:47.656
but we really have to
lean in and figure out

00:09:47.656 --> 00:09:51.176
how we can address those
systemic inequities

00:09:51.176 --> 00:09:53.976
in the platforms and tools
that students have access to,

00:09:53.976 --> 00:09:56.706
if we're gonna continue
this remote learning.

00:09:56.706 --> 00:09:58.509
- Until the census, maybe
the January administration,

00:09:58.509 --> 00:10:00.986
at least more schools will
be ready to do this type

00:10:00.986 --> 00:10:04.226
of administration, we still
don't know what might happen

00:10:04.226 --> 00:10:06.746
with some of the kids who've disengaged,

00:10:06.746 --> 00:10:09.036
that's kind of a deeper problem

00:10:09.036 --> 00:10:12.096
that everyone is facing right now.

00:10:12.096 --> 00:10:14.586
What are the things
outside, you just mentioned,

00:10:14.586 --> 00:10:16.829
essentially the digital divide access,

00:10:17.966 --> 00:10:20.066
that's a clear issue that
we've talked a lot about

00:10:20.066 --> 00:10:21.379
on this forum.

00:10:22.396 --> 00:10:24.846
What are the other
things that you're seeing

00:10:24.846 --> 00:10:28.436
that are really tough for the
education system right now?

00:10:28.436 --> 00:10:30.546
And what are you seeing
that might be categorized

00:10:30.546 --> 00:10:31.619
as silver linings?

00:10:33.096 --> 00:10:35.866
- I think one of the
challenges that we often hear

00:10:35.866 --> 00:10:37.976
from the partners that we work with,

00:10:37.976 --> 00:10:39.506
teachers who are in the field

00:10:39.506 --> 00:10:40.566
when we're doing our workshops,

00:10:40.566 --> 00:10:44.826
is they often talk about the
way the data is structured,

00:10:44.826 --> 00:10:47.406
when you're in the physical
space in the classroom,

00:10:47.406 --> 00:10:50.806
it is so much easier to be
able to run your small groups

00:10:50.806 --> 00:10:55.806
and to be able to bounce around
to the different students

00:10:56.386 --> 00:10:58.916
who need you, and when
you're in that virtual space,

00:10:58.916 --> 00:11:00.306
it is much more challenging.

00:11:00.306 --> 00:11:03.206
And especially when you think
about the kinds of tools

00:11:03.206 --> 00:11:05.626
that we have and whether or
not we're teaching whole group

00:11:05.626 --> 00:11:07.636
or small group and how
you have that set up

00:11:07.636 --> 00:11:09.576
for your students to meet.

00:11:09.576 --> 00:11:12.546
So, I think that the
scheduling is one thing

00:11:12.546 --> 00:11:16.966
that has certainly presented
a challenge to teachers.

00:11:16.966 --> 00:11:19.066
And I think the other piece is engagement,

00:11:21.134 --> 00:11:23.346
we're asking our students
and our teachers to engage

00:11:23.346 --> 00:11:25.726
in ways that haven't been the norm.

00:11:25.726 --> 00:11:29.416
And so there's this comfort
level that we also have

00:11:29.416 --> 00:11:32.286
to address, and this school
climate and community,

00:11:32.286 --> 00:11:36.086
it's one thing to build
community when you're in person

00:11:36.086 --> 00:11:37.126
with all of your learners,

00:11:37.126 --> 00:11:39.936
but to build community when
everyone is sitting behind

00:11:39.936 --> 00:11:42.976
a screen and trying to
facilitate those connections,

00:11:42.976 --> 00:11:45.816
presents a totally different challenge.

00:11:45.816 --> 00:11:48.666
And I think the silver lining
there is that what we're

00:11:48.666 --> 00:11:51.866
finding is that those
in-person techniques,

00:11:51.866 --> 00:11:56.866
work virtually as well with
obviously some modifications.

00:11:57.076 --> 00:12:01.966
But the premise of creating
that strong, connected

00:12:01.966 --> 00:12:05.605
learning team, is still the same thing

00:12:05.605 --> 00:12:06.796
that we wanna go for.

00:12:06.796 --> 00:12:10.996
And so that we know that the
foundation of all learning

00:12:10.996 --> 00:12:13.686
is strong relationships,
and I'm hearing teachers say

00:12:13.686 --> 00:12:17.266
over and over, the return on
investment that I'm getting

00:12:17.266 --> 00:12:19.786
for the way that we're
building community and climate

00:12:19.786 --> 00:12:22.226
is really paying off and
now I can really dive in

00:12:22.226 --> 00:12:24.226
to some of those deeper learning pieces.

00:12:25.156 --> 00:12:27.926
- So teachers are really
investing on this,

00:12:27.926 --> 00:12:29.436
they're on video conference, whatever,

00:12:29.436 --> 00:12:31.026
but they're making sure
that there's time for them

00:12:31.026 --> 00:12:33.153
to get to know each other,
build trust between the students

00:12:33.153 --> 00:12:35.736
and the teachers and the
students with each other.

00:12:35.736 --> 00:12:37.966
And one of the things
that's paying dividends,

00:12:37.966 --> 00:12:40.626
which makes a ton of
sense, are you seeing,

00:12:40.626 --> 00:12:43.536
pockets of teachers doing
things exceptionally

00:12:43.536 --> 00:12:44.536
well right now?

00:12:44.536 --> 00:12:47.606
Do you think there's certain
elements of what's going on

00:12:47.606 --> 00:12:50.559
right now that we should
try to retain post COVID?

00:12:51.956 --> 00:12:54.296
- You know, one of the things
that I think really stands out

00:12:54.296 --> 00:12:57.886
is there's been so much
student choice and voice

00:12:57.886 --> 00:13:00.616
that I've seen, in my own son's education

00:13:00.616 --> 00:13:04.676
as he's been remote for part
of the school year as well.

00:13:04.676 --> 00:13:08.426
And when I'm talking to
teachers across the country,

00:13:08.426 --> 00:13:13.026
when I'm partnering, so
I think one of the silver

00:13:13.026 --> 00:13:15.756
linings here is that education

00:13:15.756 --> 00:13:18.096
doesn't have to look the
same that it always has.

00:13:18.096 --> 00:13:22.466
And I think we've been given
this opportunity to innovate

00:13:22.466 --> 00:13:24.326
and to see things differently,

00:13:24.326 --> 00:13:27.026
and to know that perhaps when we come back

00:13:27.026 --> 00:13:30.156
to the brick and mortar,
in a more permanent way,

00:13:30.156 --> 00:13:32.986
that we can continue to
honor the choice and voice

00:13:32.986 --> 00:13:34.726
that we've allowed students to have,

00:13:34.726 --> 00:13:36.521
and really empower student directed

00:13:36.521 --> 00:13:41.196
and self-regulated learning in
a way that continues to meet

00:13:41.196 --> 00:13:42.126
students where they are,

00:13:42.126 --> 00:13:46.336
but also gives them that
motivational factor to engage

00:13:46.336 --> 00:13:48.346
in ways that maybe they haven't before.

00:13:48.346 --> 00:13:50.176
And I think that's the
really exciting piece

00:13:50.176 --> 00:13:52.006
that I'm seeing, is that a
lot of teachers are saying,

00:13:52.006 --> 00:13:54.646
you know what, we're learning
some really good lessons

00:13:54.646 --> 00:13:56.053
about what works for students.

00:13:56.053 --> 00:13:59.206
And we wanna make sure that
we wanna capitalize on these,

00:13:59.206 --> 00:14:01.396
and continue these
practices when we get back

00:14:01.396 --> 00:14:05.029
to what used to be the normal way.

00:14:06.236 --> 00:14:08.226
- So there's a sense that,
some of what COVID's done,

00:14:08.226 --> 00:14:11.716
is it's maybe unbundled or
personalize the experience

00:14:11.716 --> 00:14:13.736
a little bit more, whether
it's the teachers doing it,

00:14:13.736 --> 00:14:15.936
or sometimes families
have done it on their own.

00:14:15.936 --> 00:14:19.036
And obviously I believe
strongly in this as well,

00:14:19.036 --> 00:14:21.716
if we can keep a lot more
of that momentum around

00:14:21.716 --> 00:14:23.736
personalizing to the needs of students,

00:14:23.736 --> 00:14:26.096
allowing them to fill
in gaps, learn it there.

00:14:26.096 --> 00:14:27.406
I know you've written a lot about,

00:14:27.406 --> 00:14:29.336
the zone of proximal development,

00:14:29.336 --> 00:14:31.796
which is just the idea
that you should be learning

00:14:31.796 --> 00:14:34.320
the things that are just
ready for you to learn,

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:37.749
(laughs) not too easy for
you, not too hard, just right.

00:14:39.046 --> 00:14:41.179
I'm curious about the assessment,

00:14:42.026 --> 00:14:43.736
and I think a lot of folks,

00:14:43.736 --> 00:14:45.656
especially people who aren't super deep

00:14:45.656 --> 00:14:47.226
in the education world might be confused,

00:14:47.226 --> 00:14:48.616
or like there's so many assessments.

00:14:48.616 --> 00:14:51.336
We have interim assessments,
which is what a map growth is,

00:14:51.336 --> 00:14:54.116
which can measure growth,
you do it three times a year.

00:14:54.116 --> 00:14:55.926
Then you have your state assessments,

00:14:55.926 --> 00:14:57.920
which are usually done
at the end of the year

00:14:57.920 --> 00:14:59.756
and kind of give a sense.

00:14:59.756 --> 00:15:02.022
And the state assessments
are oftentimes the things

00:15:02.022 --> 00:15:07.022
that are used to measure school
performance and all of that.

00:15:08.056 --> 00:15:12.596
One, I'm curious how you
would tell everyone listening,

00:15:12.596 --> 00:15:14.276
how did these assessments fit together?

00:15:14.276 --> 00:15:16.946
And where do you think
assessment is going to go?

00:15:16.946 --> 00:15:19.186
Y'all have been doing
digital administration

00:15:19.186 --> 00:15:21.356
of the assessment, well,
it's always been digital,

00:15:21.356 --> 00:15:23.366
but you've had to do
distance administration

00:15:23.366 --> 00:15:25.546
of the assessment this
year because of COVID.

00:15:25.546 --> 00:15:26.816
You think some of that's going to stick?

00:15:26.816 --> 00:15:29.266
What assessment going to
look like in five years?

00:15:30.396 --> 00:15:31.706
- That's a really great question,

00:15:31.706 --> 00:15:32.796
and I think that it's something

00:15:32.796 --> 00:15:35.136
that we're constantly thinking about too.

00:15:35.136 --> 00:15:38.546
You know, we were one of the
first organizations to ramp up

00:15:38.546 --> 00:15:42.016
and offer our partners the
opportunity to assess remotely.

00:15:42.016 --> 00:15:46.336
We know how valuable the
assessment is, because as you said,

00:15:46.336 --> 00:15:48.876
an interim assessment is
really a snapshot in time.

00:15:48.876 --> 00:15:52.636
And I like to think of those,
I used to say Polaroids Sal,

00:15:52.636 --> 00:15:54.966
but some of the teachers I'm working with

00:15:54.966 --> 00:15:56.476
are getting younger and
younger and younger.

00:15:56.476 --> 00:15:58.906
So I have to say like, it's
an Instagram post, right?

00:15:58.906 --> 00:16:02.286
So you line up three Instagram
posts and you can look at

00:16:02.286 --> 00:16:05.936
changes over time, but
at that snapshot in time,

00:16:05.936 --> 00:16:08.806
we can really see where
students ready to learn,

00:16:08.806 --> 00:16:10.786
relative to what I'm trying to teach.

00:16:10.786 --> 00:16:12.916
And so when you think about map growth

00:16:12.916 --> 00:16:14.826
in that interim space,
we're an instructional

00:16:14.826 --> 00:16:17.346
readiness assessment,
not a mastery assessment,

00:16:17.346 --> 00:16:19.336
which is what your state summatives are.

00:16:19.336 --> 00:16:21.256
State summatives are mastery assessments,

00:16:21.256 --> 00:16:24.176
when we look at giving a
mastery test to students,

00:16:24.176 --> 00:16:26.736
we're looking at them to
get high levels of accuracy.

00:16:26.736 --> 00:16:30.126
So as many right, as they
can, because a mastery test

00:16:30.126 --> 00:16:33.266
is all about certifying
what you know and can do,

00:16:33.266 --> 00:16:36.096
relative to the standards
that are being assessed.

00:16:36.096 --> 00:16:37.676
And instructional readiness instrument,

00:16:37.676 --> 00:16:40.156
which is what map growth
is, is the opposite of that.

00:16:40.156 --> 00:16:43.126
We're actually looking
for every single student

00:16:43.126 --> 00:16:45.379
to get about half the items right.

00:16:46.286 --> 00:16:47.726
And people, that blows their minds,

00:16:47.726 --> 00:16:49.786
they're like, why would you
design a test where students

00:16:49.786 --> 00:16:52.076
are only gonna get about 50%? right?

00:16:52.076 --> 00:16:56.086
'Cause that's where the zone
of proximal development is,

00:16:56.086 --> 00:16:57.986
when we think about
instructional readiness,

00:16:57.986 --> 00:17:02.746
what we're really trying
to find is not high levels

00:17:02.746 --> 00:17:05.466
of mastery and not low levels

00:17:05.466 --> 00:17:06.996
where they have no background knowledge.

00:17:06.996 --> 00:17:09.126
We're trying to find that
sweet spot right in the middle,

00:17:09.126 --> 00:17:10.946
where they have some background knowledge,

00:17:10.946 --> 00:17:12.996
but mastery hasn't emerged yet.

00:17:12.996 --> 00:17:17.326
So, we can use that
information to create a plan,

00:17:17.326 --> 00:17:19.876
a pathway forward to mastery,

00:17:19.876 --> 00:17:22.316
because what we know
from Lev Vygotsky's work,

00:17:22.316 --> 00:17:25.046
which is where we got
this idea of the zone

00:17:25.046 --> 00:17:29.616
of proximal development, is
that when we teach in that zone,

00:17:29.616 --> 00:17:31.606
what we're doing is we're
helping move a student

00:17:31.606 --> 00:17:35.086
from where they are closing
that metaphorical gap

00:17:35.086 --> 00:17:38.896
between what they can do
and what they can't do yet.

00:17:38.896 --> 00:17:41.206
And so with an interim assessment,

00:17:41.206 --> 00:17:46.206
we at NWEA have linking studies,
where we looked at a group

00:17:46.586 --> 00:17:50.116
of students in a state who've
taken both the state summative

00:17:50.116 --> 00:17:51.876
and map growth assessment.

00:17:51.876 --> 00:17:54.526
And we do a correlational
study where we can see

00:17:54.526 --> 00:17:56.196
if this is how they score on map growth,

00:17:56.196 --> 00:17:59.065
how do they tend to score
on the state assessment?

00:17:59.065 --> 00:18:02.556
And then we can provide information
to teachers to help them

00:18:02.556 --> 00:18:05.536
understand based on a
student's map growth score,

00:18:05.536 --> 00:18:07.946
what kind of trajectory
they're on relative

00:18:07.946 --> 00:18:09.376
to that state summative.

00:18:09.376 --> 00:18:12.356
So in a way, the map
growth interim assessment

00:18:12.356 --> 00:18:15.026
becomes a predictor where
I can use that information

00:18:15.026 --> 00:18:17.096
to change a student's trajectory.

00:18:17.096 --> 00:18:21.376
So, the interim assessment
is such that it helps us

00:18:21.376 --> 00:18:23.383
monitor growth, as you said.

00:18:23.383 --> 00:18:25.516
But I like to think about how John Hattie

00:18:25.516 --> 00:18:27.056
talks about assessment and he says,

00:18:27.056 --> 00:18:29.266
we should look at
assessment as information

00:18:29.266 --> 00:18:32.506
about our impact and so
if a student is growing,

00:18:32.506 --> 00:18:34.816
we really need to figure out
what is it that we're doing

00:18:34.816 --> 00:18:37.746
that's helping get that growth
and how can I replicate that

00:18:37.746 --> 00:18:39.396
with more and more students?

00:18:39.396 --> 00:18:42.026
But if the growth isn't there, why not?

00:18:42.026 --> 00:18:44.676
What are those factors that are preventing

00:18:44.676 --> 00:18:46.836
the most growth possible forward?

00:18:46.836 --> 00:18:51.506
And how can we really get in
there and change up things

00:18:51.506 --> 00:18:53.746
before we get to that state summative?

00:18:53.746 --> 00:18:57.376
So if you think about a
balanced assessment system,

00:18:57.376 --> 00:19:00.286
we need summative assessments
to understand how students

00:19:00.286 --> 00:19:03.726
are performing relative to
those grade level expectations.

00:19:03.726 --> 00:19:06.666
We need interim assessments
that help us monitor progress

00:19:06.666 --> 00:19:09.766
along the way, but it's really
formative assessment practice

00:19:09.766 --> 00:19:11.826
that drives our day to day instruction.

00:19:11.826 --> 00:19:15.336
And so, that's one thing
that doesn't get spoken about

00:19:15.336 --> 00:19:17.666
enough when people talk about in NWEA,

00:19:17.666 --> 00:19:20.036
because they're so
familiar with our flagship

00:19:20.036 --> 00:19:24.546
product map growth, that they
don't realize how deep we are

00:19:24.546 --> 00:19:25.976
in the formative assessment space.

00:19:25.976 --> 00:19:27.686
And so that's where we really get in there

00:19:27.686 --> 00:19:30.546
and help teachers think about
their instructional practices

00:19:30.546 --> 00:19:32.896
and what am I gonna do
with that data that I get

00:19:32.896 --> 00:19:35.936
from map growth and my
classroom formative data,

00:19:35.936 --> 00:19:39.106
to keep that learning
moving forward every day

00:19:39.106 --> 00:19:42.656
between interim assessments,
so it's the macro,

00:19:42.656 --> 00:19:44.009
down to the micro.

00:19:45.466 --> 00:19:46.876
- What's interesting, I
know we've talked a lot

00:19:46.876 --> 00:19:49.616
about this as we're kind
of visioning the future,

00:19:49.616 --> 00:19:51.436
you know, you're using
the interim assessment,

00:19:51.436 --> 00:19:54.336
just explain to folks why
you actually need kids

00:19:54.336 --> 00:19:57.669
to get things wrong, is that
you're zeroing in at adapts.

00:19:59.917 --> 00:20:00.976
Let's say they're a sixth grade student,

00:20:00.976 --> 00:20:03.136
you might start them at
a fourth grade concept,

00:20:03.136 --> 00:20:04.206
if they get it right, you can give 'em

00:20:04.206 --> 00:20:05.976
a little harder concept, a little harder.

00:20:05.976 --> 00:20:07.896
Maybe you're getting to
more advanced sixth grade

00:20:07.896 --> 00:20:09.646
type concept, but then
they get something wrong

00:20:09.646 --> 00:20:13.246
and then you give them an easier question

00:20:13.246 --> 00:20:14.646
and then they get a few right,

00:20:14.646 --> 00:20:15.486
then they get something wrong.

00:20:15.486 --> 00:20:19.946
So, you're zeroing in on
exactly where that learning

00:20:19.946 --> 00:20:21.596
edge is, that zone of
proximal development.

00:20:21.596 --> 00:20:23.916
And that's also what allows
you to measure growth

00:20:23.916 --> 00:20:28.536
beyond just one grade
level, and to your point,

00:20:28.536 --> 00:20:31.706
there's formative, and you
know, when I first learned

00:20:31.706 --> 00:20:33.506
these words, I was like,
wow, there's a lot of types

00:20:33.506 --> 00:20:35.936
of assessments, but formative
is really just another way

00:20:35.936 --> 00:20:39.516
of saying, assessment in the
spirit of helping you learn

00:20:39.516 --> 00:20:43.736
how well the kids are
learning in the moment,

00:20:43.736 --> 00:20:46.746
so that the teachers or
whoever else, is trying to,

00:20:46.746 --> 00:20:51.016
even the students can better
diagnose what extra can be done

00:20:51.016 --> 00:20:52.156
to make sure they're learning it well.

00:20:52.156 --> 00:20:54.826
And what's interesting
with the map accelerator

00:20:54.826 --> 00:20:56.086
that we're doing together,

00:20:56.086 --> 00:20:58.536
obviously use the interim
assessment to figure out

00:20:58.536 --> 00:21:01.156
a personalized learning plan
that the student is ready for.

00:21:01.156 --> 00:21:04.256
But then while they're practicing
using the map accelerator

00:21:04.256 --> 00:21:07.566
on Khan Academy, that actually
also helps the teacher,

00:21:07.566 --> 00:21:09.426
inform the teacher on
what they need help in.

00:21:09.426 --> 00:21:13.396
So, it is doing some of this
kind of a shorter cycle loop,

00:21:13.396 --> 00:21:16.206
and then, every three or four months,

00:21:16.206 --> 00:21:18.016
you then get another interim assessment

00:21:18.016 --> 00:21:20.936
to understand overall how
much that's been driving

00:21:20.936 --> 00:21:23.836
the growth and then you can re-place.

00:21:23.836 --> 00:21:28.836
I say that not replace,
re-place students again.

00:21:29.116 --> 00:21:31.476
I mean, it's a fascinating
space of where we're going

00:21:31.476 --> 00:21:36.476
with assessment, we're getting
a bunch of questions here.

00:21:37.096 --> 00:21:41.466
One is well, there's families
that are worried about,

00:21:41.466 --> 00:21:43.476
actually there was a question,
I don't know where it went

00:21:43.476 --> 00:21:48.476
about parents with students.

00:21:49.266 --> 00:21:51.536
There's reports that we're
seeing an unusual number

00:21:51.536 --> 00:21:53.026
of kids who are failing this year,

00:21:53.026 --> 00:21:56.616
even kids who typically were
getting A's and B's before,

00:21:56.616 --> 00:21:58.466
any advice you would have for parents.

00:21:58.466 --> 00:22:01.986
And I guess a broader question,
and students and teachers

00:22:01.986 --> 00:22:04.176
is how do we keep kids engaged right now?

00:22:04.176 --> 00:22:05.916
And how do we keep their motivation high?

00:22:05.916 --> 00:22:09.086
So that these types of
things don't happen.

00:22:09.086 --> 00:22:11.916
- Boy, what a loaded question Sal.

00:22:11.916 --> 00:22:14.036
I have some really strong
feelings about grades

00:22:14.036 --> 00:22:15.776
and I think that that's the first question

00:22:15.776 --> 00:22:18.266
that you have to ask, what
is that grade measuring?

00:22:18.266 --> 00:22:21.256
Is that grade measuring
progress toward mastery

00:22:21.256 --> 00:22:24.406
in the subject, or is
it measuring completion

00:22:24.406 --> 00:22:25.239
of assignments?

00:22:25.239 --> 00:22:28.966
And so really having conversations
about what grades mean

00:22:28.966 --> 00:22:30.826
with teachers is I think really important.

00:22:30.826 --> 00:22:33.016
And that's the place that
teachers can create clarity

00:22:33.016 --> 00:22:34.826
with their students and with the families

00:22:34.826 --> 00:22:37.356
that they serve, is to
really help them understand

00:22:38.296 --> 00:22:42.246
what a grade means and what
they're trying to collect

00:22:42.246 --> 00:22:45.546
and to know, so that's one piece.

00:22:45.546 --> 00:22:48.326
But there's the motivational piece,

00:22:48.326 --> 00:22:50.636
our students have to understand.

00:22:50.636 --> 00:22:52.716
And this is part of that
formative assessment cycle,

00:22:52.716 --> 00:22:54.776
what it is that our expectations are.

00:22:54.776 --> 00:22:57.786
So what are we aiming for in the learning?

00:22:57.786 --> 00:23:01.696
And really give them a
pathway for understanding

00:23:01.696 --> 00:23:04.066
where we're going, but also
understanding where they are

00:23:04.066 --> 00:23:07.036
relative to where we're trying
to go and then strategies

00:23:07.036 --> 00:23:09.686
for closing the gap
between those two things.

00:23:09.686 --> 00:23:12.226
And I think when students
are empowered to see

00:23:12.226 --> 00:23:14.736
the trajectory, they're
empowered to figure out

00:23:14.736 --> 00:23:18.559
where they are, and then they
get the supportive practices

00:23:19.836 --> 00:23:21.912
in the classroom, supportive assignments,

00:23:21.912 --> 00:23:23.576
scaffolding, if you will.

00:23:23.576 --> 00:23:26.576
I've written extensively
about that on our NWEA blog,

00:23:26.576 --> 00:23:29.136
Teach, Learn, Grow, then
I think that's where

00:23:29.136 --> 00:23:30.596
the motivation comes from.

00:23:30.596 --> 00:23:33.226
A lot of learned helplessness
with students comes

00:23:33.226 --> 00:23:35.136
when they've tried and
failed, tried and failed

00:23:35.136 --> 00:23:36.276
and tried and failed.

00:23:36.276 --> 00:23:39.916
And so I like to point back
at the educational system

00:23:39.916 --> 00:23:43.486
sometimes and say, I don't wanna say

00:23:43.486 --> 00:23:45.096
we have unmotivated learners.

00:23:45.096 --> 00:23:47.006
I think that we always
have motivated learners,

00:23:47.006 --> 00:23:48.586
they may just not be motivated to do

00:23:48.586 --> 00:23:49.656
what we want them to do.

00:23:49.656 --> 00:23:52.716
So, we have to really
tap in and ask ourselves,

00:23:52.716 --> 00:23:56.576
what are we doing to help
students see their return on

00:23:56.576 --> 00:23:58.406
investment to be successful?

00:23:58.406 --> 00:24:01.296
Because that learned helplessness happens

00:24:01.296 --> 00:24:03.476
when students don't see
that return on investment,

00:24:03.476 --> 00:24:06.606
or they failed over and over after trying.

00:24:06.606 --> 00:24:09.746
So, it's important to
get early and easy wins,

00:24:09.746 --> 00:24:11.876
students have to experience success.

00:24:11.876 --> 00:24:15.506
The greatest predictor of
future success is past success,

00:24:15.506 --> 00:24:19.816
success breeds success, so
as teachers and as parents,

00:24:19.816 --> 00:24:22.136
we have to help our students get a win.

00:24:22.136 --> 00:24:24.856
And then they know what
success feels like,

00:24:24.856 --> 00:24:26.536
and then they can crave it.

00:24:26.536 --> 00:24:29.206
I always like to say, that
chocolate and red wine

00:24:29.206 --> 00:24:30.636
are two of my favorite things Sal,

00:24:30.636 --> 00:24:33.546
but I never knew to crave those
things until I tried them.

00:24:33.546 --> 00:24:35.656
And so the same thing
is true for students,

00:24:35.656 --> 00:24:37.776
we have to give them a
little taste of success

00:24:37.776 --> 00:24:39.326
before they'll want it on their own.

00:24:39.326 --> 00:24:42.076
And we might have to spoonfeed
it for a little while,

00:24:42.076 --> 00:24:44.026
and then eventually they're
gonna take the spoon away

00:24:44.026 --> 00:24:45.266
and they're gonna feed themselves

00:24:45.266 --> 00:24:46.906
because they know what it feels like,

00:24:46.906 --> 00:24:49.006
and they know what it takes to achieve it.

00:24:50.066 --> 00:24:52.626
- What I love is your statement
about there's no such thing

00:24:52.626 --> 00:24:54.867
as unmotivated students,
I hear that all the time.

00:24:54.867 --> 00:24:57.126
Oh, this might be good
for motivated students,

00:24:57.126 --> 00:24:59.246
but what about all of
these unmotivated students?

00:24:59.246 --> 00:25:01.446
Like it's in their DNA or something,

00:25:01.446 --> 00:25:05.711
and there's not a person
on the planet who is not,

00:25:05.711 --> 00:25:08.480
you know, the video game
industry has figured out

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:11.296
how to motivate people,
there's a lot of things,

00:25:11.296 --> 00:25:12.726
and I haven't met a four or five-year-old

00:25:12.726 --> 00:25:15.236
who wasn't incredibly
curious and motivated.

00:25:15.236 --> 00:25:18.216
You have to fight them off,
they wanna explore everything,

00:25:18.216 --> 00:25:19.436
they wanna ask you 10 questions.

00:25:19.436 --> 00:25:20.766
You're like, I'm done
answering your questions,

00:25:20.766 --> 00:25:23.896
please give me a break,
every four, or five-year-old

00:25:23.896 --> 00:25:25.626
in the world, and so what your point is,

00:25:25.626 --> 00:25:29.566
is that they can become
demotivated in certain domains.

00:25:29.566 --> 00:25:32.456
But once again, it's not their nature,

00:25:32.456 --> 00:25:35.256
it's just a kind of a
learned helplessness.

00:25:35.256 --> 00:25:37.286
Ideally, that doesn't
happen in the first place,

00:25:37.286 --> 00:25:40.496
but there's ways to deprogram
it by giving them some wins.

00:25:40.496 --> 00:25:42.366
We have a lab school, Khan Lab School,

00:25:42.366 --> 00:25:47.366
and there was a open house
and the perspective parents

00:25:47.606 --> 00:25:50.826
are always like, wait, this
is a school without grades.

00:25:50.826 --> 00:25:52.856
And I remind them, no, no, we have grades,

00:25:52.856 --> 00:25:55.306
we don't call them ABCDF, we could have,

00:25:55.306 --> 00:25:57.486
but we call them advanced
mastery, mastery,

00:25:57.486 --> 00:25:59.736
approaching mastery, because
they're four mastery.

00:25:59.736 --> 00:26:02.066
And the key is, is that
there's always opportunity,

00:26:02.066 --> 00:26:04.396
if a student is at approaching mastery,

00:26:04.396 --> 00:26:08.546
which you can kind of view
as a B, or even a B minus.

00:26:08.546 --> 00:26:11.206
That there's always opportunities
for them to improve it,

00:26:11.206 --> 00:26:14.056
so they never get the message
that it's somehow their innate

00:26:14.056 --> 00:26:16.856
Bness or Cness of themselves,

00:26:16.856 --> 00:26:19.126
but that's just where they are right now.

00:26:19.126 --> 00:26:20.886
If you're practicing basketball,

00:26:20.886 --> 00:26:23.246
you're a 60% free throw
shooter, it doesn't mean

00:26:23.246 --> 00:26:25.156
you're doomed to never be better,

00:26:25.156 --> 00:26:28.686
you keep practicing,
get to 80, get to 90%.

00:26:28.686 --> 00:26:31.186
So I couldn't agree with that more,

00:26:31.186 --> 00:26:33.056
I was just on a CBS
this morning yesterday,

00:26:33.056 --> 00:26:34.646
and I was like, Hey, this McKinsey study

00:26:34.646 --> 00:26:36.786
about all these kids failing classes.

00:26:36.786 --> 00:26:39.446
I was like, the key thing is
we have to give opportunities

00:26:39.446 --> 00:26:42.096
for these kids, especially
'cause they're probably failing

00:26:42.096 --> 00:26:44.786
it based on this more
completionist mindset,

00:26:44.786 --> 00:26:47.216
are they completing
the assignments or not?

00:26:47.216 --> 00:26:48.976
We have to give these kids opportunities,

00:26:48.976 --> 00:26:51.556
frankly, all kids when we
get back for them to make up

00:26:51.556 --> 00:26:54.242
the work and show that they have mastery,

00:26:54.242 --> 00:26:55.676
so that it doesn't do the opposite

00:26:55.676 --> 00:26:57.476
of what you just described.

00:26:57.476 --> 00:26:59.916
- I agree completely, and
I think what we've gotta do

00:26:59.916 --> 00:27:03.406
as an educational system
in the United States

00:27:03.406 --> 00:27:06.069
is challenge this idea
of the average as well.

00:27:06.966 --> 00:27:10.816
For me, I think that zeros are problematic

00:27:10.816 --> 00:27:12.516
and I think averages are problematic,

00:27:12.516 --> 00:27:15.236
and I think that what we're
really trying to communicate

00:27:15.236 --> 00:27:17.986
is mastery, then we
should be thinking about,

00:27:17.986 --> 00:27:20.476
then how do our grades reflect mastery?

00:27:20.476 --> 00:27:23.136
And there's an interesting
research study out there

00:27:23.136 --> 00:27:25.916
that actually shows that the
minute you put a number grade

00:27:25.916 --> 00:27:28.646
on something, the learning
stops for students.

00:27:28.646 --> 00:27:30.686
So, I always, when I'm
working with teachers,

00:27:30.686 --> 00:27:32.836
really encourage them, as long as students

00:27:32.836 --> 00:27:35.236
are in the learning cycle,
withhold that number.

00:27:36.126 --> 00:27:38.106
Think about what kind
of descriptive feedback,

00:27:38.106 --> 00:27:41.346
what kind of actionable
feedback can you provide

00:27:41.346 --> 00:27:43.126
that will feed the learning forward.

00:27:43.126 --> 00:27:46.123
And how can you make
time in your classroom,

00:27:47.506 --> 00:27:49.256
for students to use that feedback?

00:27:49.256 --> 00:27:50.506
I hear all the time from teachers,

00:27:50.506 --> 00:27:52.536
well, I write tons of
things on kids' papers,

00:27:52.536 --> 00:27:54.836
but they don't ever use it.

00:27:54.836 --> 00:27:57.606
My question is one, are
you also including a grade

00:27:57.606 --> 00:28:00.596
and two, are you making
explicit time for students

00:28:00.596 --> 00:28:02.766
to dig in and improve?

00:28:02.766 --> 00:28:04.546
And in what ways?

00:28:04.546 --> 00:28:08.376
So, if you're out there and
you're struggling as a student,

00:28:08.376 --> 00:28:11.086
or you are a parent who has
a child who's struggling,

00:28:11.086 --> 00:28:13.126
or you're a teacher who wants to dig in

00:28:13.126 --> 00:28:14.556
and think about these things,

00:28:14.556 --> 00:28:16.366
that's where it asks you to start.

00:28:16.366 --> 00:28:19.876
To really start thinking about,
what is it that I believe

00:28:19.876 --> 00:28:22.576
about grades, what is it that I believe

00:28:22.576 --> 00:28:25.486
about student learning and
what am I trying to accomplish?

00:28:25.486 --> 00:28:29.726
And how can we do that in a
way that empowers the learner

00:28:29.726 --> 00:28:33.106
to have more of the locus of
control and how do we empower

00:28:33.106 --> 00:28:38.106
the learner to set goals that
matter and are meaningful

00:28:38.236 --> 00:28:42.066
and attainable, and then how
can we be a support for that?

00:28:42.066 --> 00:28:43.966
And I really think that's
our job as parents,

00:28:43.966 --> 00:28:45.766
I think that's our job as teachers,

00:28:45.766 --> 00:28:49.086
is to help cultivate these individuals

00:28:49.086 --> 00:28:54.086
who will eventually be
empowered and can self-regulate

00:28:54.706 --> 00:28:58.386
and can take charge and
have the kinds of futures

00:28:58.386 --> 00:29:00.359
that they want for themselves.

00:29:01.892 --> 00:29:04.336
- It's a very, very powerful idea.

00:29:04.336 --> 00:29:06.426
Well, Brooke these conversations
always go much faster

00:29:06.426 --> 00:29:10.436
than I expect, thanks so much for joining.

00:29:10.436 --> 00:29:13.096
And I look forward to
going on this journey,

00:29:13.096 --> 00:29:16.966
with you and NWEA, because
I think together we can help

00:29:16.966 --> 00:29:20.676
a lot of parents, teachers
and students stay in that zone

00:29:20.676 --> 00:29:23.246
of proximal development,
understanding what's working,

00:29:23.246 --> 00:29:25.236
not working, make sure kids have a chance

00:29:25.236 --> 00:29:28.186
to master concepts, not just complete

00:29:28.186 --> 00:29:30.466
a few things without mastery.

00:29:30.466 --> 00:29:33.936
So, it's really been an honor
to talk to you about this.

00:29:33.936 --> 00:29:36.276
- Thank you Sal, I appreciate
the work and I appreciate

00:29:36.276 --> 00:29:37.726
the partnership that we have.

00:29:39.116 --> 00:29:42.676
- Thank you, well, everyone,
thanks for joining in,

00:29:42.676 --> 00:29:45.176
hopefully you enjoyed that conversation.

00:29:45.176 --> 00:29:46.596
Some really interesting
things to think about,

00:29:46.596 --> 00:29:48.080
actually, I'm gonna think a
little bit about that notion,

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:50.496
as soon as you see the number,

00:29:50.496 --> 00:29:53.346
it might in certain
ways, stop some learning.

00:29:53.346 --> 00:29:55.704
We might have to modify
some things on Khan Academy,

00:29:55.704 --> 00:29:58.636
(laughs) we'll see about that.

00:29:58.636 --> 00:30:02.396
We'll see you, tomorrow's
show, we're gonna have

00:30:02.396 --> 00:30:05.116
Beth Gonzalez, who's the
assistant superintendent

00:30:05.116 --> 00:30:06.606
of Detroit public schools.

00:30:06.606 --> 00:30:08.486
And you can imagine there's
a lot of interesting things

00:30:08.486 --> 00:30:10.806
to talk about, when
you're talking to folks

00:30:10.806 --> 00:30:13.586
who are administrators of
large public school systems

00:30:13.586 --> 00:30:15.906
like Detroit, so start
thinking of your questions

00:30:15.906 --> 00:30:18.816
and I will see you tomorrow
at 12:00 PM Pacific time,

00:30:18.816 --> 00:30:20.637
3:00 PM, Eastern.

00:30:20.637 --> 00:30:23.304
(playful music)

